# ~/.env.secrets -- machine-local credentials. NEVER committed.
#
# install.sh copies this template to ~/.env.secrets on first run and never
# overwrites it, so a re-run keeps whatever you filled in. Coder's own
# guidance for workspace secrets is a persistent file the user fills in after
# the workspace is built; this is that file.
#
# FORMAT: dotenv. One KEY=value per line. No `export`, no shell expansion,
# no quotes needed. That is the shape direnv's stdlib `dotenv_if_exists`
# reads, so a project .envrc can load the whole file when it wants to.
#
# RULES
#  - Mode is 0600. install.sh sets it; keep it.
#  - The `.env.*` rule in .gitignore already ignores ~/.env.secrets, so the
#    file cannot be committed even if it lands inside a checkout.
#  - Nothing sources this file globally, on purpose. Each consumer reads the
#    one key it needs. A global source would put a personal token into a work
#    checkout's shells and defeat the per-tree direnv split.
#  - Where a keychain exists, prefer it. resolve_gh_token() in .envrc tries
#    the keychain first and falls back here, so this file is what makes the
#    same dotfiles work on Linux and in a Coder workspace.
#  - Leave a key empty rather than deleting it. An empty key documents what
#    this machine could hold; a missing one looks like an oversight.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE GOES
#  - macOS, Linux, Coder workspace:  ~/.env.secrets
#  - WSL:                            ~/.env.secrets on the LINUX filesystem.
#    Not /mnt/c/... : DrvFs reports 0777 whatever you chmod, so a credential
#    there cannot be hidden from other Windows accounts, and reading it would
#    look exactly like reading a safe file.
#  - Windows, no WSL (Git Bash, PowerShell): %USERPROFILE%\.env.secrets.
#    The readers check $USERPROFILE when $HOME has no file. NTFS has no
#    0600, so restrict it to your account:
#      icacls "%USERPROFILE%\.env.secrets" /inheritance:r /grant:r "%USERNAME%:R"
#  - Override the location anywhere with $ENV_SECRETS_FILE.
#
# HOW TOOLS READ IT
#   bin/env-secret.sh KEY            one value, on stdout
#   bin/with-secrets.sh KEY -- cmd   run cmd with KEY exported, nothing else
# A credential reaches one process, not your whole shell.

# GitHub tokens, one per account, so a tree can pick its own owner.
# resolve_gh_token() reads GH_TOKEN_<OWNER>, uppercased, where OWNER comes
# from $GITHUB_USER. Add a line per extra account you use.
GH_TOKEN_CHESHIRECODE=

# npm publish and release auth. NODE_AUTH_TOKEN falls back to this.
NPM_TOKEN=

# --- agent CLI keys --------------------------------------------------------
# Only needed where the CLI cannot do its own browser login: WSL, Git Bash, a
# headless box, or a container. Where an OAuth login works, prefer it and
# leave these empty -- the CLIs keep those sessions in their own config, which
# is safer than a key in a file.
#
#   claude        with-secrets.sh ANTHROPIC_API_KEY -- claude
#   opencode      with-secrets.sh OPENROUTER_API_KEY -- opencode
#   cursor-agent  with-secrets.sh CURSOR_API_KEY -- cursor-agent
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
CURSOR_API_KEY=
